{"step_index":1505,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-12T01:52:35Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-12T09:52:35+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-12T09:52:44+08:00\nCreated At: 2026-11-12T09:52:35+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-12T09:52:44+08:00\nThe search for \"SQLite WAL replace database file while open in another process corruption backup API\" returned the following summary:\nReplacing or overwriting an SQLite database file on disk while it is actively open and in use by another process—especially when **WAL (Write-Ahead Logging) mode** is enabled—is a direct recipe for **database corruption or sudden application crashes**[1][2]. \n\n---\n\n### Why File-Level Replacement Fails in WAL Mode\n\n1. **Multiple Files Constitute the Database:** In WAL mode, SQLite relies on three files working in unison: `database.db` (the main file), `database.db-wal` (the write-ahead log containing recent committed transactions), and `database.db-shm` (shared memory index)[3][4]. \n2. **Stale/Mismatched WAL State:** If you overwrite only the main `database.db` file with a new version while leaving the old process running, the target process still holds an open file descriptor/handle to the *old* content, or it will look at the existing `-wal` and `-shm` files which do not match the new database pages[2][5]. This mismatch causes immediate logical corruption (`SQLITE_CORRUPT`)[6].\n3. **OS-Level Locking Behavioral Differences:** \n   * On **Windows**, you cannot overwrite an open file at all; the file replacement operation will throw a \"file in use / permission denied\" error.\n   * On **Linux/Unix**, you *can* delete or overwrite a file inode while a process has it open, but the running process keeps writing/reading the *old* unlinked file descriptor, meaning it will completely ignore your replacement file until restarted[2].\n\n---\n\n### The Correct Solutions\n\nDepending on your workflow, you should use either the **SQLite Online Backup API** or coordinate a **clean application-level restart**.\n\n#### Solution 1: Use the SQLite Online Backup API (Programmatic Replacement)\nIf you want to update/replace the contents of a live database cleanly without shutting down concurrent processes, **never overwrite files via shell commands (`cp`, `mv`, `rsync`)**. Instead, use SQLite’s built-in **Online Backup API** (`sqlite3_backup_init`, `sqlite3_backup_step`, `sqlite3_backup_finish`)[3][7].\n\nThe backup API acts *safely* across processes because it reads pages transactionally through SQLite's locking mechanism, safely handling WAL checkpoints on the fly.\n\n* **In Python (`sqlite3`):**\n  ```python\n  import sqlite3\n\n  # Open connection to the target (live) database that needs to be updated\n  dst_conn = sqlite3.connect(\"/path/to/live_database.db\")\n\n  # Open connection to your new/source database file\n  src_conn = sqlite3.connect(\"/path/to/new_replacement_data.db\")\n\n  with dst_conn:\n    # Safely copy the source database over the live destination database page-by-page\n    src_conn.backup(dst_conn)\n\n  src_conn.close()\n  dst_conn.close()\n```\n* **Via Command Line Shell (`sqlite3` CLI):**\n  ```bash\n  sqlite3 live_database.db \".restore new_replacement_data.db\"\n  ```\n  *(Note: The `.restore` command uses the Backup API under the hood, safely pushing the new state into the target database even if other readers/writers are interacting with it).*\n\n---\n\n#### Solution 2: Graceful Close, Replace, and Reopen (For Full File Swaps)\nIf your goal is to completely swap out the physical database file from an external deployment script or installer, you **must coordinate with the processes holding it open**[2]:\n\n1. **Signal the application(s)** running against the database to close their connections gracefully[2]. This forces SQLite to flush and checkpoint the `-wal` file into the main `.db` file and delete the `-wal`/`-shm` companion files[8][2].\n2. **Perform the file replacement:** Safely swap/overwrite the `.db`, `.db-wal`, and `.db-shm` files simultaneously (or just the `.db` file if you are certain no WAL file exists post-checkpoint)[1][2].\n3. **Restart the application(s)** so they open fresh file handles to the new database[2].\n\nSources:\n[1] [reddit.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEjLZ-2BDDUyTiQKP8aaJM6skddjK91gK_zxswX8T1JzO1vCFb6LxZfS8OejiQ_HlRIZ1onygoCGMqkw_SmAqbve2ohvPlWO8JTwmmQbzgxnkr7dJnpmAj2dEZS2TOHtU8EYQQB3vpQK0uBW8TVR3d7QHTfZO8IZK7UyLmZMTfoLxfub8upjlsB8t7RhjpOUluqoGg=)\n[2] [stackoverflow.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQH2e2pdMceiOE4m23kQIvvNWKSwVsSXYxVGaPdq7gKz5LV80CAvN9FlgCPIaGcV3y2ymATjUwPMfNcSSN0Kqp1xdHTTBsoSg2ljrehLTX_MAKcOSrRboZlw0b87zl-FrkswyT9Pq7A_m7BX5Zx7_QFT8zZRspOrlZPPRbbC5B_ss7XgdWWkhqa5VptV5ac=)\n[3] [dev.to](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQE4FPtazQW8whleTcuhDSQ3YoakdhLUge7YMwxOiex4dVhoXk_s4_NbMgYZg63jcimm0zq78f4NAeGInmYOJE2TmDwo3bTgOJgzrlaEgJfLjf7EmafTKQ8PVj9wHnwLws6L4DtJVfubnmBiGfgq-fvE8kuxjvcpAvEdnFKJX7I-0Z8wsKulZw-p6NYXwsL4H02umV4vce-rJBufsowM7Sa0H6WPpA==)\n[4] [coddy.tech](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGyVc8aUwPs9TYdBNUUeV5wfqT9hMwx35lG0TDh5NzKNS-ksDe33gJpf8m1PEaIfeldtMsqQCR1v1mWKJvqV9kRw2xYlfKx_JwhGWTFbjlHAlghYBHPoZLKqyHgIG1wJTBfRSPEDaTNGsDezkw=)\n[5] [sqlite.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQE6HxbPcpwsXQlruBm5T5-sNqOd-wqfgllwSZVT3Z_GnAMhhiZwdHhu4W8E9B4a45zV0kNOrmFQLJGOKOvd0EqB3OlWOacpNa2ntCL9JuUdzFMKFH91DmZZltZCg_GAbfzWnPg=)\n[6] [scottspence.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHOigwLdgPSDDSSIJd-wYiXOcJNv_JNg0I8i1qZxlu6dAmAAXH55yHNNeK3fpt8aAoZt14ROpl_dOJdjOa6DN8wE-nWYTg12vy-caIlI8hIPMpmL4Cz0zpfBKR5su3pT-WtlY9wmvJcBf5LVcxXwb8U8NVrFyRq)\n[7] [kunalganglani.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQE0qtT84jr85PSdPjBUgJynHhiu_Y0YvZLikpqQIQemsl0N5pc9_G2uIv6s-IB8iRfR8M4KlVnLbVRf5B8pJBG-_x1-i9jYr60IZgorAiQTEY2_4i0awnUiysOpKEVSILjVpDyUiW-JNxRC6pPIzB1sRw==)\n[8] [sqlite.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQG0MGlH52xVnzbFhAciVrrw3roi0lWd9ZS9jvaJE9vYs05FNPTRuLaz6rscGc8N9Kyc44HUhJydBpG8kIfY-CIokWPNfPDog38QrMVUmmbfA9FH-_k=)\n\nNote: Source URLs are HTTP redirects; check where they resolve if you need the direct link."}
{"step_index":1506,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-12T01:52:35Z","content":"Created At: 2026-11-12T09:52:35+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-12T09:52:50+08:00\nCreated At: 2026-11-12T09:52:35+08:00\nCompleted At: 2026-11-12T09:52:50+08:00\nThe search for \"SQLite FTS5 INSERT OR REPLACE DELETE transaction concurrency busy_timeout WAL mode Windows\" returned the following summary:\nWhen combining **SQLite FTS5**, heavy concurrent writing (`INSERT OR REPLACE`, `DELETE`), **WAL mode**, and **Windows file-locking semantics**, developers frequently hit `SQLITE_BUSY` (\"database is locked\") errors[1][2]. \n\nFTS5 tables are complex virtual tables[3] that internally maintain multiple index b-trees (terms, postings, columns). An `INSERT OR REPLACE` (which translates to a `DELETE` followed by an `INSERT`) or an explicit `DELETE` causes intense write amplification. Under high concurrency, this triggers lock contention[4].\n\nThe following architectural blueprint addresses these specific failure modes on Windows.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Core Issues on Windows & FTS5\n* **Windows File Locking (POSIX vs. Win32):** Unlike Linux, Windows enforces strict file-lock semantics via the Win32 API. If a reader or checkpoint operation has a lock handle open, or if multiple handles fight over the `-wal` / `-shm` files, Windows bubbles up sharing violations more aggressively as `SQLITE_BUSY`.\n* **Deferred Transaction Upgrades (The Silent Deadlock):** If multiple threads/connections start a transaction with a plain `BEGIN` (which is *deferred*), they acquire a read lock. When they subsequently try to execute an `INSERT`/`DELETE`, they attempt to escalate to a write lock simultaneously. If two connections try to upgrade at the same time, one gets blocked and a deadlock or immediate timeout occurs[5].\n* **FTS5 Write Weight:** FTS5 updates write to multiple internal segments. Long transactions keep write locks active longer, starving other threads[2].\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Solution Checklist\n\n#### A. Order Your PRAGMAs Correctly (Crucial for Cold Starts)\nIf you configure your database upon opening, ensure `busy_timeout` is set **before** `journal_mode = WAL`. If you set `journal_mode = WAL` first, a cold initialization on an empty/new database can race across threads and fail instantly with `database is locked` before the timeout is even armed[6].\n\n```python\n# Python Example\nimport sqlite3\n\nconn = sqlite3.connect(\"search.db\", timeout=5.0)  # Sets underlying busy timeout\ncursor = conn.cursor()\n\n# 1. Set timeout explicitly first (in ms)\ncursor.execute(\"PRAGMA busy_timeout = 5000;\")\n\n# 2. Enable WAL mode next\ncursor.execute(\"PRAGMA journal_mode = WAL;\")\n\n# 3. Recommended performance pragmas for concurrent workloads\ncursor.execute(\"PRAGMA synchronous = NORMAL;\")\ncursor.execute(\"PRAGMA temp_store = MEMORY;\")\n```\n\n#### B. Use `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` for Write Transactions\nNever use a plain `BEGIN` (Deferred) if you intend to modify the FTS5 table. Plain transactions only acquire locks lazily when the first write statement is executed, opening the door to concurrency collisions. \n\nInstead, force an immediate exclusive write lock reservation[5]:\n```sql\n-- Correct way to start a write transaction\nBEGIN IMMEDIATE;\n\n-- Perform your FTS5 operations safely\nINSERT OR REPLACE INTO my_fts_table(rowid, title, body) VALUES (1, '...', '...');\nDELETE FROM my_fts_table WHERE rowid = 2;\n\nCOMMIT;\n```\nIf another thread is already writing, `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` will safely wait up to the `busy_timeout` (e.g., 5000ms) instead of throwing an immediate concurrency crash[7].\n\n#### C. Batch Your `INSERT` / `DELETE` Operations\nFTS5 performs significantly better when operations are batched. Avoid opening a separate transaction for every single row insertion or deletion.\n* **Bad:** 1,000 individual transactions calling `INSERT OR REPLACE` sequentially. (This creates 1,000 distinct WAL file syncs and massive lock contention).\n* **Good:** Wrap 500–1,000 changes inside a single `BEGIN IMMEDIATE ... COMMIT` block.\n\n#### D. Connection Architecture (The Single-Writer Pool)\nEven in WAL mode, SQLite permits **only one writer at a time**[7][1]. \n* **Readers:** Can scale infinitely and concurrently without blocking writers[7][1].\n* **Writers:** Must be serialized at the application level. \n\nIf you are using multi-threaded environments (like Python's `concurrent.futures`, Node.js, C#, or Go), implement a dedicated **Write Queue or Write Lock mutex** at the application layer so that only one thread attempts a `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` write transaction at any given moment[8]. \n\n#### E. Handle FTS5 Auto-Optimization (`automerge`)\nFTS5 automatically merges internal b-tree segments over time (`automerge`). During heavy write bursts, an automatic background merge can coincide with your `INSERT OR REPLACE` / `DELETE` calls, intensifying `SQLITE_BUSY` spikes. \n\nIf you notice intermittent locking during massive ingestion spikes, you can temporarily tune or disable FTS5's automatic merging behavior during the bulk load phase, and run an explicit optimization command manually afterward[3]:\n```sql\n-- Disable/adjust internal merging if needed during heavy loads, \n-- or run a manual optimize command during low-traffic windows:\nINSERT INTO my_fts_table(my_fts_table) VALUES('optimize');\n```\n\nSources:\n[1] [phenomic.io](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFQZV03Npa2tD8Jae3ESZA-BZSF25B2lecWjxUOBWTyoff_dAy_WblynKZtHmobSSDvv3pMR_zdWDT0LviQbhgqQlV1fbiMH5dXfPxHih4jhr7DxSpiC704DNnjFe8ConOmV_twxCLxOd7r-5I5lcTrYhtlaF8lFdFH)\n[2] [dbpro.app](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFWVNX1dKfaRnO6SMVNaRJN0JoyxY7uOBekMA5ocbEvDVP0tOcrMGNI8hz0pdRbOhyaQHGzeyWPqmBuWhOqntoGyIzXqoQXECVoy_6NuL9MADPxXhRrn5vM6K4J_aVMLRa-6IgpeLPl74ifFukzxdM=)\n[3] [sqlite.org](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGFiK9jOP7hDrvPo_UoqSixcGflhnmZPRJVVZUUpTJW7Tj9QWBeuPepX_u6HTxtegUWBSzW3YREzrE8Q_CniBuOpqJToUq3atSO0WfVvK9otpqH)\n[4] [github.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQEhZvid5k8dOSAIC3DX2C-3Eqb05wNIbem3W3s0EA5Fe48Rj-yZBpxxDgiP8veXQKnSXkde9I8ung5wqM5t2RYnMA70GGiJOBknw10HRp299pXrnlM5cjnyV6sl__a1N4o5nEIUVQ==)\n[5] [berthub.eu](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQHGZd5sAXttRryEroaVBEk1-7jGw382-fxwDzYYmpTU91zVdpLA2ZPopAgAs6_9iYmumY9h-y-cCxTUIE-QA5qJLJhxjI1Hsj1BM_ObuG8XuxVbDa2G3ca3Iwinmv4Bn6IN9A8rKQ2vbGcw6xZwFdMvM4zjOZy9GXYzokRKMvADQbqTeMCtUYbRhJ3_zxzpJBM=)\n[6] [cursor.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFa6Kp5YN7SgyVLwzf_QrnVfycmqKGAnt8Q102wk96LAixnNmWsixZJ5PN1HGvSqffgFkA2VfG4Y10-qQ60E5YrytRuQHRYHMDrbBouIoUIQJSIKK1YR-TubfzuAmFe-eZ169XsL9OO9KlM9Ng0OBc6iuT02vQTc8h79I1aKSYG_gajVTrfR3Kxe6zQlQkcpNVgax0eUJoSSe-heQ4GSymzpXUMu6cdzSbvVIiDA4iZqXN-9VFsEQkKQImPCjP26zhVuVImajg80KlJHaxWbjtT8voXP1p_ErF59kCB1AoGkYGnz2ANpu_v)\n[7] [reddit.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQFzrJpcij1rgcKhZonBklMy1SIGeoCAUGwR_dQ8zEstkhKbX1Ou-mKfUYFd9DqOxkU7PDoTyGkbxWvWoPaEWW73LM3Q6S2FtCLzjZyKnPA2gGV6fOzdxHTr0kkWRMlJsRvkHvQqB7s_zF0NKAFDePQnX-nXj6xL-HqG8qp_6YDs0rfSn_-mQ-4Xsq677zO6QDBds_nZwuE=)\n[8] [stackoverflow.com](https://vertexaisearch.cloud.google.com/grounding-api-redirect/AUZIYQGNsAv94Xu0nF6NFJjsZAOsj7BFBbQGo89Xaa5_wjTBLNMP5r4y2zPPpipkTf3VKSaHCYocpul4ra0jA68MZxrxaBaHsrneEQp77q4mjD3iWTxvdWwRXOLYAHKLmUiKsEosW2FU1wyrT2lRTUzwvXrnxMsv7qkEWYlkCTlJ56Kx3owJvmjeWmGLfQ==)\n\nNote: Source URLs are HTTP redirects; check where they resolve if you need the direct link."}
